Gleki, as far as I understood from the docs, those platforms are (or will=
=20
be) open sourced. This is not really the issue actually. Indeed, this=20
(more-or-less) open source code is used as a foundation layer, acting ontop=
=20
of a blockchain to replace "trusted" 3rd parties. This is the same=20
principle with cryptocurrencies, but here instead of using blockchain=20
transactions for *money* (replacing the bank as a trusted 3rd party),=20
"smart contracts" and other (non-financial) "validated transactions" are=20
used as a base for software (named "dApps"). Said otherwise, you will still=
=20
have to finance the blockchain construction & validation, even if source=20
code is open.

( I hope my explanation is correct! )

Cheers,

Sukender

